2. Scope and Unit of Analysis

2.1 Theoretical Scope

This is a theoretical paper. It does not:

· perform empirical estimation,
· conduct physical experiments,
· train machine-learning models,
· collect new data,
· claim historical proof.

It provides definitions, a consistent mathematical architecture, formal propositions where possible, and falsifiable hypotheses that can guide future research.

2.2 Primary Unit of Analysis

The primary unit of analysis is the country-level society:

i \in \mathcal{A}

where \mathcal{A} denotes African societies. Each society is indexed by time t. The central variable is:

C_i(t) = \text{civilizational capability of society } i \text{ at time } t.

The framework can later be extended to subnational research clusters or city-regions.

2.3 Continental Statements as Distributions

Africa is not treated as a single analytical unit. Rwanda, Nigeria, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Botswana differ more in energy, institutions, education, and connectivity than many intercontinental country pairs.

Continental statements are therefore summaries of a distribution:

\mathbf{ACPC}_{\mathcal{A}}(t) = \{ ACPC_i(t) : i \in \mathcal{A} \}.

The appropriate descriptive statistics are the median, interquartile range, and upper tail, not a single “African” index.

---

3. Core Conceptual Distinctions

3.1 Civilizational Capability Is Not GDP

GDP measures market production. Civilizational capability includes the ability to understand, create, coordinate, reproduce, and expand knowledge and infrastructure over time.

A society may have high GDP but weak scientific autonomy. Another may have lower GDP but specialized scientific capability. The framework therefore uses a broader capability function.

3.2 Productive Viability and Human Viability

Two dimensions are kept separate.

· Productive viability V_P: the capacity to generate scientific, technological, industrial, and institutional output.
· Human viability V_H: the capacity of a society to reproduce human relationships, culture, meaning, and intergenerational transmission.

A technocratic policy could raise V_P while collapsing V_H. The framework explicitly forbids treating such an outcome as civilizational success.

3.3 Hard and Soft Constraints

Some capacities function as hard constraints: without a minimum threshold, other capacities cannot be realized. Reliable electricity, basic institutional trust, and connectivity are examples.

Other capacities are soft constraints: above hard thresholds, they can partially substitute for one another. Stronger research institutions may partially compensate for weaker technological infrastructure, for example.

This distinction is formalized in Section 5.

3.4 AI Augmentation Is Not Substitution

Artificial intelligence can amplify human cognition or replace it. These are not the same mechanism.

The model keeps AI outside the substitutable soft-capacity aggregator. AI enters through AI-augmented human capital. This preserves the distinction between machine productivity and human capability.

