5. Aggregation Architecture

5.1 Hard Gates

Let the hard capacities be:

\mathcal{K}_i = \{E_i, I_i, X_i\}.

For each hard capacity K_k, define a smooth gate:

g_k(K_k) = \frac{K_k^{\rho_k}}{K_k^{\rho_k} + K_{k,50}^{\rho_k}}
\tag{1}

where:

· K_{k,50} is the half-saturation constant: the value at which g_k = 0.5;
· \rho_k > 1 controls the sharpness of the gate.

As \rho_k \to \infty, the gate approaches a step function.

The hard viability threshold is defined separately as the value at which the gate passes 1-\alpha, with \alpha \in (0,1) chosen by the researcher:

K_{k,\min} = K_{k,50} \left(\frac{1-\alpha}{\alpha}\right)^{1/\rho_k}.
\tag{2}

For future empirical work, \alpha = 0.05 is a natural default.

5.2 Soft Capacities

Let the soft capacities be:

\mathcal{S}_i = \{H_i^{AI}, S_i, T_i, R_i\}
\tag{3}

where H_i^{AI} is AI-augmented human capital, defined in Section 7.

The soft aggregator is:

\Phi_i(\mathcal{S}_i) = \left( \sum_{j \in \mathcal{S}} \omega_{ij} S_{ij}^{\rho_S} \right)^{1/\rho_S}
\tag{4}

with:

\sum_{j \in \mathcal{S}} \omega_{ij} = 1

and:

\rho_S \le 1, \quad \rho_S \ne 0.

The elasticity of substitution is:

\sigma_S = \frac{1}{1 - \rho_S}.

Limiting cases:

· \rho_S \to 1: near-perfect substitutes.
· \rho_S \to 0: Cobb-Douglas, \Phi_i = \prod_{j \in \mathcal{S}} S_{ij}^{\omega_{ij}}.
· \rho_S \to -\infty: Leontief/min.

5.3 Combined Capability Function

Total civilizational capability is:

C_i(t) = \Phi_i(\mathcal{S}_i) \cdot \min_{k \in \mathcal{K}} g_k(K_{ik}).
\tag{5}

This formulation has two immediate consequences.

1. If any hard capacity is far below its threshold, the minimum gate is near zero, and total capability collapses. This preserves the bottleneck principle.
2. Above hard thresholds, soft capacities substitute imperfectly through the CES function.

The use of \min_k g_k(K_{ik}) rather than a product of gates is deliberate. A product would impose that joint moderate weakness across several hard constraints compounds catastrophically. A weakest-link formulation reflects the idea that the most binding constraint determines effective capacity.

5.4 Weakest-Link Derivative

Let:

G_i(K) = \min_{k \in \mathcal{K}} g_k(K_{ik})

and let:

k_b = \arg\min_{k \in \mathcal{K}} g_k(K_{ik})

be the binding hard constraint.

Proposition 1 (Weakest-link elasticity).
Where differentiable, the elasticity of civilizational capability with respect to a hard input K_k is:

\frac{\partial \ln C_i}{\partial \ln K_k}
=
\begin{cases}
\rho_k [1 - g_k(K_{ik})] & \text{if } k = k_b, \\
0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\tag{6}

Proof.
If k is the binding constraint, then:

\ln C_i = \ln \Phi_i + \ln g_k(K_{ik}).

Differentiating with respect to \ln K_k:

\frac{\partial \ln g_k}{\partial \ln K_k}
=
\rho_k
\left[
1 - g_k(K_{ik})
\right].

For any non-binding hard input k \ne k_b, the minimum function is locally unaffected by small changes in K_k, so the derivative is zero. \square

This property formally distinguishes the weakest-link architecture from a product-of-gates specification. Only the binding constraint contributes to the marginal elasticity.

