6. Human Capital Conversion Efficiency

6.1 Sequential Transition Probabilities

Let:

HCCE_i(t) = \frac{H_i(t)}{P_i^{work}(t)}

where P_i^{work} is the working-age population.

The conversion from population to advanced human capital proceeds through sequential stages:

H_i = P_i^{work} \cdot \pi_{i1} \cdot \pi_{i2} \cdot \pi_{i3}
\tag{7}

where:

· \pi_{i1}: probability that a school-age individual completes basic education with sufficient literacy and numeracy;
· \pi_{i2}: probability that a basic-educated individual attains advanced secondary or tertiary scientific/technical education;
· \pi_{i3}: probability that an advanced-educated individual remains engaged in research, engineering, or high-complexity work rather than leaving for non-technical occupations or migrating.

Innovation output is treated separately:

\pi_{i4} = \frac{\text{technologically significant innovations}}{\text{researchers}}.

Note that \pi_4 is not a transition probability. It is an innovation-yield ratio and should not be included in the same bottleneck chain as \pi_1,\pi_2,\pi_3.

6.2 Bottleneck Targeting

Proposition 2 (Bottleneck targeting under equal marginal cost).
Let:

HCCE = \pi_1 \pi_2 \pi_3.

Assume that the marginal policy cost of increasing any transition probability \pi_j is identical and constant for j = 1,2,3. Then the marginal increase in raw advanced human capital per unit of policy investment is largest for:

j^* = \arg\min_{j \in \{1,2,3\}} \pi_j.
\tag{8}

Proof.
For any j:

\frac{\partial HCCE}{\partial \pi_j}
=
\prod_{k \ne j} \pi_k
=
\frac{HCCE}{\pi_j}.

Because HCCE > 0, the derivative is strictly decreasing in \pi_j. Therefore the largest derivative corresponds to the smallest \pi_j. Under the equal marginal cost assumption, the largest derivative also corresponds to the largest return per unit of investment. \square

This proposition is conditional. If marginal costs differ across transitions, the ranking may reverse.

