8. Human-Centered Constraint

8.1 Productive Viability

Productive viability is:

V_{P,i}(t) = C_i(t).
\tag{17}

A society must maintain:

V_{P,i}(t) \ge V_{P,\min}

to preserve a minimum level of scientific, technological, institutional, and infrastructural capacity.

8.2 Human Viability Vector

Human viability is not one number. It is a vector:

\mathbf{v}_{H,i} = (v_{1,i}, v_{2,i}, v_{3,i}, v_{4,i}, v_{5,i}, v_{6,i})
\tag{18}

with each component normalized to [0,1].

Component Indicator Measurability
v_1 Fertility relative to replacement Operationalizable now
v_2 Social trust Operationalizable now
v_3 Mental health and social well-being Partially operationalizable
v_4 Family and community participation Partially operationalizable
v_5 Cultural and linguistic transmission Partially operationalizable
v_6 Sense of meaning and future orientation Aspirational

8.3 Hard Human-Viability Constraint

The human-centered condition is:

\min_k v_{k,i}(t) \ge v_{k,\min}.
\tag{19}

This is a Leontief constraint. A society with excellent scientific output but collapsing fertility, trust, or mental health is not counted as civilizational success.

The thresholds v_{k,\min} are not assumed in this theoretical paper. They must be set explicitly in future empirical work. In particular, v_{1,\min} should be defined by explicit demographic modeling rather than as an ad hoc fertility floor.

8.4 Objective Function

The civilizational objective is:

\max_{\mathbf{u}_i} \; C_i(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{u}_i)
\tag{20}

subject to:

V_{P,i} \ge V_{P,\min}

and:

\min_k v_{k,i} \ge v_{k,\min}.

This can be stated compactly as:

\boxed{
\max \; \text{Human-Centered Civilizational Capability}
}

