10. Falsifiable Hypotheses

The following hypotheses are designed for future regression or historical analysis. All falsification criteria are stated for pre-specified 95% confidence intervals, not point estimates.

H1 — Human Capital Bottleneck Hypothesis

Claim. In African societies that have already achieved near-universal basic education, the most binding educational transition is \pi_2 or \pi_3, not \pi_1.

Null hypothesis. All educational transitions are equally binding.

Falsification criterion. If no systematic difference is found between transition probabilities after controlling for income, H1 is rejected.

H2 — Hard-Gate Hypothesis

Claim. Below critical energy, institutional, or connectivity thresholds, increases in education do not significantly raise civilizational capability.

Null hypothesis. Education has the same marginal effect across all energy and institutional levels.

Falsification criterion. If the interaction between education and hard constraints is non-negative and significant, H2 is weakened.

H3 — Conditional AI Equalizer Hypothesis

Claim. Above hard thresholds, AI augmentation produces larger relative gains for initially weaker scientific systems.

Formally:

\frac{\partial^2 \ln Y_i}{\partial A_i \partial S_{i0}} < 0,
\tag{23}

where Y_i is scientific output and S_{i0} is baseline autonomous scientific capacity.

Null hypothesis.

\frac{\partial^2 \ln Y_i}{\partial A_i \partial S_{i0}} \ge 0.

Falsification criterion. If the interaction term in log output is non-negative, H3 is rejected.

H4 — AI Dependency Hypothesis

Claim. Beyond a threshold D_{\text{crit}}, imported AI dependence reduces autonomous scientific capacity, even if measured scientific output remains positive or grows.

Formally:

\frac{\partial \dot{S}_i}{\partial D_i} < 0 \quad \text{for } D_i \ge D_{\text{crit}},

while:

\frac{\partial \ln Y_i}{\partial A_i} > 0

may still hold.

Null hypothesis. AI dependence has no negative effect on autonomous scientific capacity.

Falsification criterion. If no negative threshold effect is found after controlling for current output, H4 is rejected.

H5 — Human Viability Hypothesis

Claim. Sustained violation of the human viability constraint reduces future civilizational capability through demographic or intergenerational transmission failure.

Null hypothesis. Human viability has no long-run effect on civilizational capability after controlling for productivity.

Falsification criterion. If long-run capability remains high despite sustained v_k < v_{k,\min}, H5 is rejected.

H6 — Multiplanetary Participation Hypothesis

Claim. African societies that develop autonomous scientific, industrial, and energy capacity are more likely to become durable contributors to multiplanetary infrastructure than societies participating only through symbolic or dependency-based projects.

Null hypothesis. Symbolic participation predicts long-run multiplanetary capability as strongly as autonomous capacity.

Falsification criterion. If future historical or model-based analysis finds no difference, H6 is rejected.

