## Appendix D. Dynamical-Systems Analysis

### D.1 The Full State-Space System

The complete system (Eq. 24) collects:

$$
\dot H_i = P_i^{work}\cdot\frac{d}{dt}\left(\pi_{i1}\pi_{i2}\pi_{i3}\right) + \pi_{i1}\pi_{i2}\pi_{i3}\dot P_i^{work} \tag{D.1}
$$
$$
\dot D_i = \nu_i\left[D_i^*(t) - D_i(t)\right] \tag{D.2}
$$
$$
\dot S_i = a_iH_i^{AI} + b_iR_i - \gamma_iD_i^{\eta_i}S_i - \mu_iS_i \tag{D.3}
$$

together with policy- and exogenous-driven laws of motion for $E_i, T_i, I_i, R_i, X_i$ that are left unspecified at this theoretical stage (they enter through the control vector $\mathbf{u}_i$ and exogenous vector $\mathbf{z}_i$, Section 11.2–11.3 of the main text).

### D.2 Local Stability of the $(D_i,S_i)$ Subsystem

Treating $A_i$ as a slow-moving or exogenous parameter over the relevant horizon, the $(D,S)$ subsystem (dropping the $i$ subscript) is
$$
\dot D = \nu\left[D^*-D\right], \qquad
\dot S = a H\Gamma(A,D) + bR - \left(\gamma D^\eta+\mu\right)S.
$$

**Proposition D.1 (local stability).** If $D^*$ is treated as locally constant (i.e. $F,L$ change slowly relative to $\nu$), the subsystem has a unique fixed point $(D,S)=(D^*, S^*(D^*))$ with $S^*(D^*)=N(D^*)/M(D^*)$ as in Appendix C.3. The Jacobian at this fixed point is
$$
J = \begin{pmatrix} -\nu & 0 \\ \dfrac{\partial \dot S}{\partial D} & -M(D^*) \end{pmatrix},
$$
which is lower-triangular with eigenvalues $-\nu<0$ and $-M(D^*)<0$ (since $M(D^*)=\gamma (D^*)^\eta+\mu>0$ whenever $\mu>0$). Hence the fixed point is locally asymptotically stable for any admissible parameter values.

**Proof.** Direct computation: $\partial\dot D/\partial D = -\nu$, $\partial \dot D/\partial S = 0$, $\partial \dot S/\partial S = -M(D^*)$, and $\partial \dot S/\partial D$ is some finite value (given in Lemma C.4) that does not affect the eigenvalues of a triangular matrix, whose eigenvalues are its diagonal entries: $-\nu$ and $-M(D^*)$. Both are strictly negative under $\nu>0$ and $M(D^*)>0$, so by the Hartman–Grobman theorem the fixed point is locally asymptotically stable. $\blacksquare$

**Interpretation.** Local stability of $(D^*,S^*)$ means the system does not explode; it converges to whatever steady state is implied by the *current* foreign/local AI capability mix. The dependency trap (Proposition 3) is therefore not a statement about instability — it is a statement about an **adverse shift in the stable equilibrium itself** as the slow-moving driver $D^*$ (governed by the evolving ratio $F/(F+L)$) trends upward over time. This distinction — stable-but-worsening equilibrium, rather than runaway instability — is the correct reading of the "trap" terminology in Section 7.4 and Section 11.5(3).

### D.3 Time-Scale Separation

Because $D_i$ adjusts toward a *moving* target $D_i^*(t)$ (Eq. 13–14) rather than a fixed constant, the full dynamics are non-autonomous. A standard simplification is time-scale separation: if $\nu_i$ (dependency adjustment speed) is fast relative to the rate of change of $F_i, L_i$ themselves, $D_i(t)\approx D_i^*(t)$ (quasi-static approximation), and the reduced system becomes
$$
\dot S_i \approx a_iH_i\Gamma_i(A_i, D_i^*(t)) + b_iR_i - \left[\gamma_i(D_i^*(t))^{\eta_i}+\mu_i\right]S_i,
$$
a single non-autonomous ODE in $S_i$ driven by the exogenous/policy path $D_i^*(t)$. This reduction is offered as a tractable special case for future empirical calibration, not as a claim that $\nu_i$ is always large.

### D.4 Feedback-Loop Formalization (Section 11.5)

Each qualitative loop in the main text corresponds to a sign pattern in the Jacobian of the full system:

| Loop | Sign pattern | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| Education–research–innovation | $\partial \dot H/\partial R > 0$ (via funding), $\partial \dot R/\partial H>0$ | Positive feedback, reinforcing |
| AI–research | $\partial \dot S/\partial A > 0$ (via $H^{AI}$), $\partial \dot L/\partial S>0$ (local capability grows with research) | Positive feedback, reinforcing |
| Dependency trap | $\partial \dot D/\partial (\text{imported use}) > 0$, $\partial \dot S/\partial D<0$ (Prop. 3 region) | Negative feedback on $S$, positive on short-run $Y$ |
| Human viability | $\partial \dot H/\partial \mathbf{v}_H < 0$ below threshold (via fertility/trust channels, not modeled explicitly in the state equations) | Delayed negative feedback |

The first two loops are reinforcing (positive feedback dominates locally); the latter two can generate stagnation or decline even while short-run indicators improve, which is the structural reason the architecture treats productive and human viability as *separate*, jointly binding constraints (Section 8) rather than substitutes.

