### E.3 Parameter Identification Strategy (Specification Only)

For future empirical work, the following identification mapping is proposed:

| Parameter | Candidate identification strategy |
|---|---|
| $K_{k,50}, \rho_k$ | Nonlinear least squares or maximum likelihood fit of gate functions to cross-country panel data, using $C_i$ proxies (e.g. patent output, scientific publication counts) as the dependent variable, with hard inputs as regressors — subject to the caveat that $C_i$ itself is not directly observed and must be proxied |
| $\omega_{ij}, \rho_S$ | CES share regressions (log-linearized around a reference point) or GMM estimation exploiting relative price/cost variation across soft capacities, where available |
| $\theta_i^{local},\theta_i^{import},\delta_i$ | Difference-in-differences or event-study designs around discrete AI-adoption shocks, distinguishing domestically-developed from imported AI tools where usage logs or procurement records permit |
| $\gamma_i,\mu_i,\eta_i$ | Estimated from the discretized Euler equation of (15), $S_{t+1}-S_t = a H^{AI}_t+bR_t-(\gamma D_t^\eta+\mu)S_t$, via panel regression with country and time fixed effects |
| $\nu_i$ | Estimated from the autocorrelation structure of observed $D_i(t)$ series, using the discretized form of (13) |

All of the above are specification proposals; none are estimated in this paper (Section 2.1).

