### E.5 Complexity and Well-Posedness Summary

| Component | Functional form | Convexity/curvature | Range |
|---|---|---|---|
| Gate $g_k$ | Logistic (Eq. 1) | Sigmoidal (Lemma B.1.4) | $(0,1)$ |
| Soft aggregator $\Phi_i$ | CES (Eq. 4) | Concave/quasi-concave for $\rho_S\le1$ (Lemma B.3.5) | $\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$ |
| Combined capability $C_i$ | Product of $\Phi_i$ and $\min_k g_k$ | Neither globally convex nor concave (product of a concave and a min-of-sigmoids) | $\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$ |
| AI modifier $\Gamma_i$ | Truncated power-law difference (Eq. 10) | Non-monotonic in $A_i$ possible (augmentation vs. displacement) | $[\underline\Gamma,\infty)$ |
| Dependency $D_i$ | First-order linear ODE toward moving target (Eq. 13) | Linear, globally stable for fixed target (Lemma C.7) | $[0,1]$ |
| Scientific capacity $S_i$ | Linear ODE with state-dependent decay (Eq. 15) | Linear in $S_i$ for fixed $D_i$; steady state as in Prop. 3 | $\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}$ |

Because $C_i$ is not globally concave (it is a product of a concave function and a $\min$-of-sigmoids, neither of which combination is concavity-preserving in general), the maximization in Eq. (20) is **not guaranteed to have a unique interior solution** under arbitrary control paths $\mathbf{u}_i$; future empirical/optimization work should treat this as a global (not merely local) optimization problem, or restrict attention to regions of the state space where local concavity can be established (e.g. away from gate inflection points, per Lemma B.1.4).

