## Appendix F. Statistical Specification for Hypotheses H1–H6

For each hypothesis, this appendix states a minimal testable regression/estimation form consistent with the falsification criteria in Section 10. These are specifications, not estimates.

**H1 (Human Capital Bottleneck).** For societies with $\pi_1 \ge \bar\pi_1$ (near-universal basic education), test
$$
H0: \beta_{\pi_2} = \beta_{\pi_3} \quad \text{in} \quad \ln HCCE_{i,t} = \beta_{\pi_1}\ln\pi_{1,i,t}+\beta_{\pi_2}\ln\pi_{2,i,t}+\beta_{\pi_3}\ln\pi_{3,i,t}+\mathbf{X}_{i,t}'\boldsymbol{\delta}+\epsilon_{i,t},
$$
via a Wald test on $\beta_{\pi_2}-\beta_{\pi_3}$, with $\mathbf{X}_{i,t}$ including income controls, at 95% confidence.

**H2 (Hard-Gate Hypothesis).** Estimate
$$
\ln C_{i,t} = \beta_1 \ln H_{i,t} + \beta_2 (\ln H_{i,t}\times \mathbb{1}\{G_{i,t}<G_{thresh}\}) + \mathbf{X}_{i,t}'\boldsymbol{\delta}+\epsilon_{i,t},
$$
and test $H0: \beta_2 \ge 0$ against $H1:\beta_2<0$ (education's marginal effect is *suppressed* below the hard-gate threshold).

**H3 (Conditional AI Equalizer).** Estimate
$$
\ln Y_{i,t} = \beta_1 A_{i,t} + \beta_2 S_{i,0} + \beta_3 (A_{i,t}\times S_{i,0}) + \mathbf{X}_{i,t}'\boldsymbol{\delta}+\epsilon_{i,t},
$$
and test $H0:\beta_3\ge0$ against $H1:\beta_3<0$, corresponding to Eq. (23).

**H4 (AI Dependency Hypothesis).** Using panel data on $D_{i,t}$ and estimated $S_{i,t}$, estimate a threshold regression
$$
\Delta S_{i,t} = \beta_1 H^{AI}_{i,t} + \beta_2 R_{i,t} + \beta_3\left(D_{i,t}\times \mathbb{1}\{D_{i,t}\ge D_{crit}\}\right) + \mathbf{X}_{i,t}'\boldsymbol{\delta}+\epsilon_{i,t},
$$
with $D_{crit}$ estimated via a Hansen-type threshold search, and test $H0:\beta_3\ge0$ against $H1:\beta_3<0$, while separately confirming $\partial\ln Y/\partial A>0$ still holds in the same sample (joint test, not a single coefficient).

**H5 (Human Viability Hypothesis).** Using lagged panel data, estimate
$$
C_{i,t+\tau} = \beta_1 C_{i,t} + \beta_2 \mathbb{1}\{\min_k v_{k,i,t} < v_{k,\min}\} + \mathbf{X}_{i,t}'\boldsymbol{\delta}+\epsilon_{i,t},
$$
for a future horizon $\tau$ (e.g. 10–20 years given demographic transmission lags), and test $H0:\beta_2=0$ against $H1:\beta_2<0$.

**H6 (Multiplanetary Participation Hypothesis).** This hypothesis is inherently long-horizon and likely requires historical/comparative-case analysis rather than panel regression at present, given the absence of a sufficient sample of societies with realized multiplanetary infrastructure participation. A provisional specification, to be revisited as data accumulate, is a survival/hazard model:
$$
h_i(t) = h_0(t)\exp\left(\beta_1 S_{i,t} + \beta_2 T_{i,t} + \beta_3 E_{i,t} + \beta_4\,\text{Symbolic}_{i,t}\right),
$$
where $h_i(t)$ is the hazard of achieving a defined durable-participation milestone, and $H0:\beta_4 \ge (\beta_1+\beta_2+\beta_3)/3$ (symbolic participation predicts as strongly as autonomous capacity) is tested against $H1$ that autonomous-capacity terms dominate.

All tests use pre-specified two-sided or one-sided 95% confidence intervals as stated in Section 10; no post-hoc threshold selection is permitted under the falsification protocol.

